# Install a service from source

Clone the project, install its declared language dependencies, then run `trellis update` and `trellis generate`. The CLI reads only native `.trellis` source and canonical installed artifacts; it never executes TypeScript or Rust authoring code.

For local API dependencies, use a `path` entry in `trellis.toml`. Registry dependencies remain exact and digest-verified through `trellis.lock`.

## Build a reproducible consumer

Use `trellis update` when intentionally changing dependency resolutions. For an
existing deployment checkout, use `trellis install` to reproduce its lock, then
`trellis generate` to build its private language SDKs. Run the project's own
format, check, test, and build commands before deployment. Do not edit generated
artifacts or rewrite imports as an installation step.

## Establish deployment authority

Log in to the target runtime with `trellis login <url>`. Create the service
deployment, apply its generated canonical API and participant artifacts, and
provision an instance using `trellis svc <id> create`, `apply`, and `provision`.
Review the requested boundary before accepting authority. See
[installing an image](/guides/install-service-from-image) for the artifact flags
and provisioning sequence; source and image deployments use the same authority
model.

## Run and update

Store the returned private identity seed securely and configure the service's
documented Trellis URL, deployment ID, and instance ID. Start its normal run
command. Bootstrap resolves its resource handles; application code must not
inject NATS administrator credentials or binding payloads.

For an update, rebuild from the new IDL and source, apply the matching artifacts,
review any pending authority change, then restart the service. Keep the same
provisioned identity for the same instance. Do not reprovision merely because
the generated SDK or application binary changed.
